{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Learning Rate.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Learning Rate.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"learning rate learning rate","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Learning Rate.md","fileName":"Learning Rate.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Learning Rate","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-15 02:20\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Neural Network Training]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Neural Network Training","Gradient Descent","Model Weight Space","Hyperparameter","deeplearning_mit.epub"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Learning Rate.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Learning Rate.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"learning rate learning rate","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Learning Rate.md","fileName":"Learning Rate.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Learning Rate","heading":"Learning Rate","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"The learning rate scales how far a training rule changes model weights in response to an error or gradient. With [[Gradient Descent]], a large rate takes bigger steps through [[Model Weight Space|weight space]]; a small rate takes smaller ones.\n\nKelleher explains the tradeoff using a ball rolling toward a hole: steps that are too large may pass a useful minimum or make training unstable, while very small steps can make progress slow. The rate is a [[Hyperparameter]] chosen for the training process, not a connection weight learned by the ordinary weight-update rule. Some schedules start larger and reduce it as training progresses.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Neural Network Training","Gradient Descent","Model Weight Space","Hyperparameter","deeplearning_mit.epub"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Learning Rate.md::2","sourceKey":"6 - Full N